8440031,2024-02-28,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Problem with a company's investigation into an existing problem,Their investigation did not fix an error on your report,"Failed To Comply With FCRA for Identity Theft Dispute - 4th Request In XX/XX/XXXX, I, XXXX XXXX, notified all three credit bureaus that I was a victim of identity theft. Despite this initial notification, I did not receive a response from the credit bureaus. Subsequently, on XX/XX/XXXX, I filed a formal complaint with you regarding the undisputed items on my credit report that I do not recognize and have not applied for. 

XXXX Complaint ID : XXXX Equifax Complaint ID : XXXX XXXX Complaint ID : XXXX In my complaint, I provided substantial evidence to support my claim of identity theft. This evidence included a copy of my identity theft report, a police report documenting the incident, and my personal identification details. I have included them to this complaint also. 

I specifically requested that the disputed fraudulent items be promptly removed from my credit report in accordance with the provisions outlined in the 15 U.S. Code 1681c2. This code stipulates that if an individual provides proof of their identity, an identity theft report, and a statement asserting that the information in question does not pertain to any transactions they have made, then the disputed items must be deleted within four business days of receiving the dispute. 

Not only did I adhere to these requirements by submitting the necessary documentation, but I also included a copy of my police report to further substantiate my claim of identity theft. 

The Credit Bureaus deleted a few items but always provide a generic response that they need additional time and will respond within 60 days. According to the FCRA, when you dispute information on your credit report, the credit bureaus are required to conduct a reasonable investigation within 30 days of receiving the dispute. This investigation may be extended by 15 days under certain circumstances, making the total time frame for resolution 45 days with a traditional dispute but as a victim of identity theft this does not apply to me and items must be deleted and blocked permanently within 4 days. 

